# ★ THESE TWO TREES ARE NOT COMMITTED, AND THE REASON IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THEM.
#
# Each is a small filesystem tree that the runtime-check differential probes. One case in the
# predicate tree is "a directory that EXISTS AND IS EMPTY", another is "a path that is a FILE used
# as a directory". Git cannot track an empty directory at all, so committing the tree would need a
# placeholder file inside it — and that file would destroy the case it was meant to preserve: the
# directory would no longer be empty, `directoryHasFiles` would return true, and the case would keep
# passing while testing nothing.
#
# So the SHAPE lives in the fixture JSON (written by the same generators that recorded the
# expectations), and both sides build the tree from it: the TypeScript generator before it executes
# the real module, and the Go test before it compares. One description, two consumers — and a fresh
# clone builds exactly the tree the expectations were recorded against, because it is the same list.
#
# Verified by deleting both trees and running the suite: it rebuilds them and passes. That is the
# check a new clone performs, and tools/parity/README.md records the failure it prevents.
runtimecheck-tree/
runtimecheck-probe-tree/
# Same reasoning, same shape: the own-docs corpus contains a HIDDEN DIRECTORY (`.hidden-dir`) and a
# hidden FILE, which are cases the walker is supposed to skip. Committing them would need a file
# inside the hidden directory, which is exactly what the case asserts must not happen.
owndocs-tree/
